# Untitled

## Targeting cancer cells through Mn(II)–dpa grafted silica nanoparticles
\(^{1}\)School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Jiangsu University, Zhenjiang 212013, China  \(^{2}\)Department of Radiology, the Affiliated Hospital of Jiangsu University, Zhenjiang 212001, China  \(^{3}\)School of Pharmacy, Jiangsu University, Zhenjiang 212013, China
The unique properties of paramagnetic nanoscale metal-organic frameworks provide them with high potential as key probes and vectors in the next generation of biomedical applications. To increase the nanoparticle targeting at the tumor site, the grafting of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa (dpa = di(picolyl)amines) or oxide nanoparticles \((\mathrm{SiO}_{2})\) is proposed. The new \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles can enhance the MR imaging area in cancer tissues and perturb the \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\)-loaded mitochondria swelling. Experimental results indicate the cancer cells may be targeted through possible intracellular \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) signaling mitochondria accumulating in vivo.
\(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{II})\) -dpa, silica nanoparticles, mitochondria, calcium
## 1 Introduction
Mn (II) complexes are attractive as potential MRI contrast agents since they have relatively high electronic spins (5/2), fast water exchange rates and high in vivo longitudinal (r1) MR relaxivities by binding to intracellular proteins [1, 2]. Similar to \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) and unlike the lanthanide, manganese is a natural cellular constituent, and often a cofactor for enzymes and receptors. Mn (II) ions are much less toxic than \(\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}\) ions. Mn(III)-labeled nanobials and manganese-based nanoscale metal-organic frameworks are reported as a magnetic resonance imaging [2, 3]. To increase the nanoparticle targeting at the tumor site, surface modified multifunctional nanoparticles (MFNPs) have shown great promise as new probes for cancer-specific biomedical imaging and carriers for drug delivery [4–6]. The magnetic nanocrystal-antibody probe system [5] and antibiofouling polymer-coated superparamagnetic iron oxide nanoparticles [7] were used as in vivo cancer imaging in live mice.
Mitochondria play a central role in cell life and death, and are known to be important in a wide range of disease, including cancer, diabetes, cardiovascular disease, etc. [8]. The unique structure and functional characteristics of mitochondria enable the selective targeting of drugs designed to modulate the function of this organelle for therapeutic gain [9]. Mitochondria can accumulate \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) ions through an ATP-dependent Ca transporter [10].
The enhanced uptake of \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) in cancer cells makes it possible to design cancer-targeted compounds through Ca-transport. The antitumor active dinuclear \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) complexes with N-allyl di(picolyl)amine can inhibit the swelling of over calcium loaded mitochondria [11]. The Mn (II) ion is a longitudinal relaxation time (T1) shortening magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) contrast agent that enters cells such as cardiomyocytes or pancreatic \(\beta\)-cell through voltage-gated calcium channels [12]. Transitional metal complexes of di(picolyl)amine with reversible coordinated bond were used to recognize proteins [13]. Therefore, the complex of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa (dpa = di(picolyl)amines) modified \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\)-\(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa nanoparticles could be new kinds of nanoparti-
cles to target the cancer cells through intracellular \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) signaling mitochondria accumulating in vivo.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Instruments and materials
TEM was performed at room temperature on a JEOL JEM-200CX transmission electron microscope using an accelerating voltage of \(200\;\mathrm{kV}\). FT-IR characterizations were performed using a Nicolet Nexas 470 FT-IR Spectrophotometer in the spectral range of \(4000{-}400~\mathrm{cm}\). The electronic absorption spectrum was recorded using a UV-2450 UV-Vis Spectrophotometer at room temperature. The content of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) ions in the nanoparticles was measured by Vista ICP-OES. In vivo MR images were performed using Siemens Trio \(3.0\;\mathrm{T}\) MR imager at room temperature.
\(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) (negatively charged silica sol Ludox HS-40 (Sigma) (composition: \(40\%\) \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\), \(60\%\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), surface area \(\sim220\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), \(1.3\;\mathrm{g/mL}\) at \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)), 3-aminopropyltrimethoxysilane (APS), ethanol, triethylamine, dichloromethane, 2-chloromethyl pyridine hydrochloride, manganese chloride \(\mathrm{(MnCl_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O)}\), methanol and toluene were used without further purification. Water was purified with a Millipore Milli-Q system.
## 2.2 Synthesis of nanoparticles
## 2.2.1 Preparation of APS-grafted nanoparticles
\(100\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles was dispersed by sonication in \(20\;\mathrm{mL}\) anhydrous toluene at \(40\;\mathrm{kHz}\), \(70\;\mathrm{W}\) for about \(3\;\mathrm{min}\). \(0.8012\;\mathrm{g}\) freshly distilled APS was added and maintained under rapid stirring for \(2\;\mathrm{h}\). The suspension was then refluxed for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) to achieve the reaction. The nanoparticles were then washed and purified by centrifugation three times with ethanol and then water to remove unreacted APS. The samples were dried at \(70\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under low pressure for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\). IR (KBr) \((\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\): \(\mathrm{v}(\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{H})\) 3409 s, \(\mathrm{v}(-\mathrm{CH}_{2}-)\) \(2946\;\mathrm{m}\), \(\mathrm{v}\) (Si-O) \(1098\;\mathrm{s}\).
## 2.2.2 Preparation of Mn-(dpa)-grafted nanoparticles
The APS-grafted nanoparticles prepared above were dispersed in \(20\;\mathrm{mL}\) of dichloromethane, and \(1.1830\;\mathrm{g}\) 2-Chloromethyl pyridine hydrochloride and \(0.7306\;\mathrm{g}\) triethylamine were added. The suspension was then refluxed for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\), then \(0.48\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}\) was added. \(10\;\mathrm{h}\) later, the excess of unreacted \(\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}\) was removed by filtration. The filtrate was reduced to \(5\;\mathrm{mL}\) on a rotary evaporator, and then dissolved in methanol. Acetone was added to get the solid particles, and the particles were dried at \(70\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under low pressure for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\). The amount of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) ions was evaluated by ICP after the nanoparticles were cooked with nitric acids at \(70\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\). The content of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) in the nanoparticles is \(3.25\%\). IR (KBr) \((\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\): \(\mathrm{v}(-\mathrm{CH})\) \(3040\;\mathrm{m}\), \(\mathrm{v}(-\mathrm{CH}_{2}-)\) \(2929\;\mathrm{m}\), \(\mathrm{v}(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C},\;\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N})\) \(1604\;\mathrm{s}\), \(1481\;\mathrm{ms}\), \(\delta(\mathrm{CH},\) pyridine) \(767\;\mathrm{s}\), \(\mathrm{v}\) (Si–O) \(1098\;\mathrm{s}\). UV-Vis \(\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{nm})\): 214, 259.
