## 2.3 In vivo MR imaging in rats
Magnetic resonance imaging experiments were carried out on the male Wistar model rats \((170{-}200\;\mathrm{g})\) bearing ascites carcinoma for each sample using Siemens Trio \(3.0\;\mathrm{T}\) MR imager at room temperature. The rats were anesthetized via intraperitoneal injection of \(10\%\) urethane solution at \(1.0\;\mathrm{mL}\) \(/100\;\mathrm{g}\) body weight dose. T-weighted images were measured instantly after injection of the complex and persisted for \(120\;\mathrm{min}\). MRI signal intensity enhancement was monitored up to \(90\;\mathrm{min}\) post-contrast injection. Multislice and multiecho sequence was with repetition time (TR) of \(300\;\mathrm{ms}\) and echo time (TE) of \(13.6\;\mathrm{ms}\). Imaging of the axial view had a field of view of \(50\times50\;\mathrm{mm}^{2}\), a slice thickness of \(1.5\;\mathrm{mm}\), a matrix size of \(256\times224\), and a NEX of \(3\).
## 2.4 Mitochondrial swelling
Liver mitochondria were isolated by conventional differential centrifugation from adult rats. The livers were homogenized in \(250\;\mathrm{mM}\) sucrose, \(1\;\mathrm{mM}\) EGTA, and \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) Hepes buffer \((\mathrm{pH}\;7.4)\). The mitochondrial suspension was washed twice in the same medium containing \(0.1\;\mathrm{mM}\) EGTA, and the final pellet was resuspended in \(250\;\mathrm{mM}\) sucrose to a final protein concentration of \(80{-}100\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\). Mitochondria \((0.4\;\mathrm{ng}\) of protein) were incubated in \(1.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) of a medium containing \(125\;\mathrm{mM}\) sucrose, \(65\;\mathrm{mM}\) KCl, \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) HEPES-KOH, \(\mathrm{pH}\;7.4\), and \(5\;\mathrm{mM}\) potassium succinate \((+\;2.5\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\) rotenone) at \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Test compounds were dissolved in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and diluted with culture media. Various concentrations of tested complexes were added to the assay mixture. Swelling was initiated by the addition of \(50\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}\) to the sample cuvette at \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Mitochondrial swelling was estimated from the decrease in absorbance at \(540\;\mathrm{nm}\) measured by a Hitachi U-2000 Spectrophotometer. The extent of mitochondrial swelling was assayed by measuring the decrease in absorbance \((A)\) at \(540\;\mathrm{nm}\) and the inhibitory rate of mitochondrial swelling was calculated as follows: \((\Delta A_{\mathrm{control}}-\Delta A_{\mathrm{drug}})/\Delta A_{\mathrm{control}}\times100\%\), \(\Delta A=\Delta A_{\mathrm{0min}}-A\).
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Synthesis and characterization of nanoparticles
Surface modification of silica nanoparticles with biocompatible \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) is potentially beneficial to the preparation of MR contrast agents for in vivo applications. The schematic representation of the surface modification of silica nanoparticles is shown in Scheme 1.
FT-IR spectra of the APS-grafted silica nanoparticles confirmed the existence of amino in the purified nanoparti-
cles, showing characteristic peaks around 3409 and 2946 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) that correspond to stretching bands of \(\mathrm{N-H}\) and \(\mathrm{C-H}\), respectively. The characteristic peaks of the \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles around 3040, 2929, 1604, 1481, 1018 and \(767\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), correspond to stretching bands of \(\mathrm{C-H}\), \(\mathrm{C-H}\), \(\mathrm{C=C}\), bending vibration in the plane of \(\mathrm{C-H}\) and the out-of-plane bending of \(\mathrm{C-H}\) of the pyridyl groups, respectively, confirming the existence of the pyridyl groups in the purified nanoparticles. The strong peaks around \(1098\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) also confirmed the existence of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\), which corresponds to stretching band of \(\mathrm{Si-O}\). These nanoparticles are soluble in water.
## 3.2 In vivo imaging
The accumulation of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles as an in vivo MR contrast agent was evaluated using a \(3.0\;\mathrm{T}\) scanner. Upon intraperitoneal injection of \(10\;\mathrm{mg/kg}\) body weight of the nanoparticles, a significant T1-weighted enhancement is clearly visible around the cancer cells of a Wistar rat bearing ascites carcinoma in oxter \(34\;\mathrm{min}\) post injection in axial and coronal views (Figure 2). The signal intensity in cancer cells was increased by \(90\%{-}200\%\) within \(34{-}120\;\mathrm{min}\) post-injection. Maximum enhancement at \(101\;\mathrm{min}\), followed by a plateau-like course until at least
\(120\;\mathrm{min}\) after administration of the contrast agents, was observed indicating the selective accumulation of the nanoparticles within cancer cells. It is interesting to find that nanoparticles enter cancer tissues forming an outside cyclic shapes MR imaging. The enhanced MR imaging area in cancer tissues indicates that the \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles can enter into cancer cells selectively in vivo.
## 3.3 Interaction with mitochondria
The interaction of the \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparti
cles (nMn) with mitochondria was further studied by measuring the \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\)-loaded mitochondrial swelling. The \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\)-loaded mitochondria swelling was enhanced in the presence of \(1.69{-}3.38~\mathrm{mg}\) (the content of the Mn (II) ions is in the range of \(50{-}100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\)) Mn(II)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles (Figure 3), which indicates the interaction between \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles and mitochondria exists [14]. Mitochondria are involved in the maintenance of intracellular \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) homeostasis. One of the features of tumor cells is their dependence on glycolysis for ATP generation, which induces the release of \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) from the endoplasmic reticulum and leads to enhanced uptake of \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) by mitochondria. The \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles increased the \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\)-loaded mitochondrial swelling indicating that this \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles might interfere with the \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\)-transport system. However, the swelling of \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\)-loaded mitochondria was inhibited completely in the presence of \(100~\upmu\mathrm{M}~\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}\). Experimental results indicate the \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles interact with mitochondria in a way different from \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) ions. The mechanism for the activation of the \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) uptake by \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles needs further investigation.
It is the \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa complex modified on the nanoparticle surface that enhances its specific interaction with cancer cells. Nanoparticles with sizes of \(60{-}70~\mathrm{nm}\) may escape from the renal filtering elimination and accumulate at the tumor after prolonged circulation. The \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles show the selective tumor accumulating
targeting through the \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) signaling and passive targeting.
## 4 Conclusions
A new kind of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\)-dpa-grafted silica nanoparticles has been prepared, which offers a new strategy for the development of multifunctional nanoparticles to target cancer cells through intracellular \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) signaling mitochondria accumulating in vivo for MRI detection. The nanoparticles can be used potentially to monitor cancer metabasis in vivo.
